\subsection*{2. Laziness}

A lazy consumer pulls values from an iterator only when needed. This
section covers user-written wrappers (\texttt{take}), the built-in lazy
consumers (\texttt{zip}, \texttt{map}, \texttt{filter},
\texttt{enumerate}), the \texttt{itertools} module, and pipelines that
chain them together.

\subsubsection*{2.1 Take: Generator Wrapping a Generator}

\texttt{take(g, n)} is a generator that pulls exactly \texttt{n} values
from another generator \texttt{g}. Because both are generators, state
(the position within \texttt{g}) is shared: consuming through
\texttt{take} also advances \texttt{g}.

If \texttt{g} runs out before \texttt{n} items are taken,
\texttt{next(g)} raises \texttt{StopIteration}, which propagates out of
\texttt{take}.
